      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Peripheral neuropathy in diabetes begins as a physiologic aberration related to hyperglycemia and its subsequent effects of endoneurial hypoxia, elevated sorbitol levels, and decreased myoinositol levels. Resultant decreases in sodium-potassium-adenosine triphosphatase levels ultimately lead to structural alterations at the nodes of Ranvier. Aldose reductase inhibitors and dietary myoinositol supplementation are being used in long-term clinical studies to monitor the possibility that they may prevent or reverse these abnormalities. In the meantime, symptomatic treatment remains the mainstay of management."},
